_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:518:`range_bound` must stop at the delimiter `as`. Ordinary expr also consumes `as` casts; the unrestricted expansion `until expr as IDENT` is **not LL(1)**. **OPEN-19** recommends a bound expression mode with top-level casts disabled and casts allowed in parentheses. Example recommendation: `borrow a from lo until (n as u64) as v {…}`. This restricts only an undecided delimiter edge; it does not relabel the decided `as`.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:526:Slice 25 declares C bindings as `extern fn`, optionally `trusted extern fn`, explicit `with(...)`, mandatory semicolon and no body. `#[link_name = "symbol"]` and `#[export_name = "symbol"]` payload forms are recommendations (OPEN-15), not an inferred `extern "C" {…}` block. Direct calls only: foreign function values and `extern "C" fn` types are not admitted ([OD4](../docs/decisions.md#od4)). FFI-safe types exclude enums ([OD110](../docs/decisions.md#od110)); C structs cross by value at every size ([OD122](../docs/decisions.md#od122)). `kept buf: H`, no marker meaning call-only, and the paired receipt discipline are already decided by the [full Q3.12 row](../docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json). The submit contract names a paired extern taking exactly Kept<H> and returning H; borrow-derived pointers are forbidden there. A kept raw pointer is not a substitute for the owned allocation. OPEN-23 concerns remaining raw API qualification and transcription, not these settled choices. Semantic validity obligations must not be replaced by inventing an `unsafe {…}` source block.
