# Modules and Names

A program is a tree of modules, one source file per module, loaded by `mod name;`. Every
declaration is private to its module unless it is declared `pub`, and another module reaches it
only by importing it (`import a.{f, T as U};`). Names resolve once, before checking, to exactly
one declaration. This chapter covers name resolution (MOD-1), visibility (MOD-2), one binding
per name in a module (MOD-3), loading module files (MOD-4), the privacy of `std.libc` (MOD-6),
generated interfaces (MOD-7, pending), calls the compiler generates (MOD-8) and what an import binds
(MOD-9). There is no MOD-5: the number was never allocated, and IDs are not renumbered
([MAINTAINERS.md](MAINTAINERS.md) §2.1). Representation privacy (which fields and constructors another module
may use) is TYPE-11, and trait coherence is the traits chapter.

OD67 refuses locals that reuse a visible top-level function, import alias, constant or intrinsic name (SCOPE-1 and SCOPE-2). TIGHTENING_DECISIONS
D9 (one source file per module with `pub` as its interface, and interface artifacts the compiler
generates and checks) is decided and scheduled late; it is MOD-7, pending R-0556. The design record is
[VISIBILITY_AND_MODULE_HYGIENE.md](../archive/design-records/VISIBILITY_AND_MODULE_HYGIENE.md).

## Rules

<a id="MOD-1"></a>
### MOD-1. Every name resolves to one declaration

**MOD-1@1.** Every name a program uses resolves to exactly one declaration visible where it is used (a local binding, a declaration of the module, an import, a type parameter or a builtin), and a name that resolves to none, a type alias that names itself, and `Self` outside an `impl` are refused.

- Kind: refusal
- Uses: none
- Codes: E0100 (resolve), E0101 (resolve), E0102 (resolve), E0103 (resolve), E0104 (resolve), E0105 (resolve), E0106 (resolve), E0107 (resolve), E0108 (resolve), E0109 (resolve), E0112 (resolve), E0125 (resolve), E0200 (check), E0201 (check), E0202 (check), E0263 (check), E0267 (check)
- Status: enforced
- Enforced by: Concrete.Pipeline.resolve
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: resolution by identity (`DefId`, `LocalId`), never by spelling (R-0492)
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** Resolution validates every name once, before checking. Later passes do not yet
work only with what a name denotes: Elab and CoreCheck still look some names up by spelling, and
R-0492 moves them to identities. Bugs 039 to 045 were names resolved more than once, differently; binders
are now keyed by identity (SCOPE-1, SCOPE-3), and declarations get the same treatment under R-0492.
The checker repeats some lookups (E0200 to E0202, E0263, E0267) as defense in depth.
The builtins include the enums `Option` and `Result`, which a module uses without an import; OD43 refuses user declarations that collide with these builtin type names. The types chapter's
[builtin catalog](types.md#builtin-functions) records the current compiler's builtin functions,
their signatures, the capabilities it checks and how it handles their names.

```con reject name=undeclared-name code=E0100 stage=resolve pair=declared-name
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    return missing + 1;
}
fn main() -> u8 {
    if example_value() == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

```con accept name=declared-name exit=42
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    let present: i64 = 41;
    return present + 1;
}
fn main() -> u8 {
    if example_value() == 42 {
        return 42;
    }
    return 1;
}
```



<a id="MOD-2"></a>
### MOD-2. Only `pub` declarations cross a module boundary

**MOD-2@1.** A declaration is private to its module unless it is declared `pub`, and another module uses it only by importing it from a module that exists, where it is `pub`.

- Kind: refusal
- Uses: none
- Codes: E0110 (resolve), E0111 (resolve), E0126 (resolve), E0284 (check), E0285 (check), E0323 (check), E0418 (elab), E0419 (elab), E0423 (elab)
- Status: enforced
- Enforced by: Concrete.Pipeline.resolve, Concrete.importRefusal
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: none
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** Private by default makes every public API intentional: a missing `pub` cannot
export a helper by accident. `pub` on a type exports its name, not its representation (TYPE-11).
There is one public level today; a package-internal `pub(pkg)` is designed for when packages
depend on packages. Resolve, Check and Elab each verify imports (E0111, E0285, E0419).

**Codes by case.** Each of the three refusals has one code per pass that can report it, Resolve
first and Check or Elab when they meet the import again (`importCode` and `unimportableCode` in
`Concrete/Resolve/FileSummary.lean`): an unknown module is E0110 (resolve), E0284 (check) or E0418
(elab); a declaration that is not `pub` is E0111, E0285 or E0419; and an import naming a public
trait, which no import binds, is E0126, E0323 or E0423. On a single file the import refusals are
reported at resolve (E0110, E0111, E0126); a qualified call to a private function there is E0101
(unknown function).

Visibility has two levels, `pub` and private, and private is the default, so a module's public
surface is explicit and nothing is exported by accident; a `pub(pkg)` level is deferred until
packages need it, and `pub(super)` or `pub(in path)` will not be added (restructure the modules
instead). There are no glob imports, permanently: every imported name is listed, so each module
begins with a manifest of what it uses and a name's origin is always visible (STDLIB design
principle 8). A module's non-public items are not in its `FileSummary.publicNames` and cannot be
named in an import list (E0111). Capabilities are not hidden by a module boundary: calling a
function needs the capabilities in its signature (TRUST-4), so a module cannot smuggle a `File`
call behind an `Alloc` signature, and each public function declares only what it needs; std contains
`Unsafe` behind `trusted` functions that expose domain capabilities (operations need their declared resource and permission).
`--report caps`, `--report authority` and `--report interface` show these surfaces, and a package
policy (`[policy] deny = ["Unsafe"]`) can refuse capabilities. The visibility design and its
pre-package hygiene checklist are the archived
[VISIBILITY_AND_MODULE_HYGIENE.md](../archive/design-records/VISIBILITY_AND_MODULE_HYGIENE.md)
(several of its first-release choices, such as all fields and variants visible, were since replaced
by TYPE-11 and R-0011).

An import binds a name in the importing module only and does not make it `pub` there, so a module
cannot re-export what it imports, and `pub import` is a parse error (SYN-1).

```con reject name=import-private code=E0111 stage=resolve pair=import-public
// spec-file: main.con
mod a;
import a.{hidden};
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    return hidden();
}
pub fn main() -> u8 {
    if example_value() == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// spec-file: a.con
fn hidden() -> i64 {
    return 1;
}

```

```con reject name=submodule-imports-private code=E0285 stage=check pair=import-public
// spec-file: main.con
mod b;
mod a;
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    return b::g();
}
fn main() -> u8 {
    if example_value() == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// spec-file: a.con
fn secret() -> i64 {
    return 42;
}
array_tail    = ";", INT, "]" | array_list_tail ;
array_list_tail = "]" | ",", expr, array_list_tail ;
path_tail     = ["::", path_after_separator] ;
path_after_separator = arguments, ["::", IDENT, path_tail]
                     | IDENT, path_tail ;
constructor_body = "{", [field_value, field_value_tail], "}" ;
field_value_tail = [",", field_value_after_comma] ;
field_value_after_comma = field_value, field_value_tail | ε ;
field_value   = IDENT, ":", expr ;
value_else    = value_block
              | "if", head_expr, value_block, "else", value_else ;
```

All expression auxiliaries inherit the slices of their operators/atoms from section 2. `path_tail` and arguments arrive at 11 for generics, 14 for associated/trait calls, 17 for module paths, and 10 for `Enum::Variant`; simple calls at 5. The proposed path production parses a common neutral path and defers declaration classification; it does not approve arguments on arbitrary intermediate module segments. Those restrictions and explicit generic enum constructors are **OPEN-14/20**. The syntax distinguishes `f::<T>(x)` from `x < y`, with a single-token choice after `::`: `<` starts arguments, IDENT continues the path. Actual calls to a function value become legal only at slice 20.

`head_expr` is the same precedence hierarchy with a separate **head primary**: at enclosing `(`/`[` depth zero it has no constructor-body suffix. Entering grouping, call parentheses, array brackets or index brackets restores ordinary expr for the contents. Thus `if LIMIT {…}` ends the head, while `if test(P { x: 1 }) {…}` permits a literal inside parentheses. `as` also terminates the source of a range borrow, so that source must use the place grammar below rather than unrestricted expr. [OD16](../docs/decisions.md#od16), [OD34](../docs/decisions.md#od34) fix the head rule, not a casing convention.

`attributes` before a postfix are checked to contain only expression-legal `may_not_terminate` permissions, target an indirect call, and attach to that entire immediately following postfix, not the next binary operand ([no-hidden-traps](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-no-hidden-traps)). The enclosing statement parser consumes a leading attribute prefix only once; it hands the same prefix to either loop or expression classification. Nested attributes inside arguments are parsed by expr itself. Attachment to parenthesized postfix chains, duplicate expression prefixes and interaction with casts are **OPEN-15/16** for the exact frontend judgment.

The place sublanguage, used for assignment checks and borrow-source parsing, is proposed as:

```ebnf
place         = place_base, {place_projection} ;
place_base    = IDENT | "(", place, ")" | "*", place_base ;
place_projection = ".", IDENT | "[", expr, "]" ;
```

Place identity is structural; not every syntactic place is valid for every type. [OD121](../docs/decisions.md#od121), [audit projection rule](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit) require a local or projection through a place; bind call results before projecting. No tuple `.0` remains after [OD54](../docs/decisions.md#od54). Heap opening has its own scoped form, not `(&h).f` ([OD36](../docs/decisions.md#od36)); no automatic receiver borrow ([OD32](../docs/decisions.md#od32)): use `(&s).m()` or `(&mut s).m()`. Precise grouped/dereferenced place precedence and method-call versus projection treatment are **OPEN-20**.

| Atom/form | Authority / OPEN detail |
|---|---|
| value if, value match, block tails | [OD77](../docs/decisions.md#od77), [SYN-8/9](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-8), [STMT-8](../legacy/docs/spec/statements.md#STMT-8); value if requires else; OPEN-16 |
| constructors `S { f: e }`, `E::V { f: e }` | enum separator [OD77](../docs/decisions.md#od77), argument-based enum inference, otherwise explicit [OD129](../docs/decisions.md#od129); struct literal shell slice 1; explicit expression fields recommended under OPEN-20; empty struct literal already admitted by OD65 and approved slice 1 |
| array `[a,b]`, `[e; N]` | repetition evaluated once, Copy [OD149](../docs/decisions.md#od149); empty list literals refused by TYPE-7; separators and allowed length OPEN-14/20 |
| `()` | [OD66](../docs/decisions.md#od66), [OD157](../docs/decisions.md#od157) |
| `Trait::method(&x, ...)` | [OD6](../docs/decisions.md#od6), [audit](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit) |
| multilevel module path | [OD151](../docs/decisions.md#od151); imports required [OD139](../docs/decisions.md#od139); name classification [OD152](../docs/decisions.md#od152) |
| borrowed expression `&place` / `&mut place` | [OD1](../docs/decisions.md#od1), [OD2](../docs/decisions.md#od2); parse unary syntax then reject non-place/temporary roots |
| `*r`, `*h`, `*p` | reference 13, heap 19, raw 24; [OD59](../docs/decisions.md#od59), [OD13](../docs/decisions.md#od13); semantics depend on type; raw non-Copy move-out must use read_owned |

No free-standing block expression has been added merely because a value branch has a block. Whether standalone `{…}` is a primary or statement is **OPEN-16**. No tuples, closures, anonymous functions, named call arguments, variadics or operator overloading are inferred from general-purpose expression notation.

codex
1: Reject; naming collisions should not force module splits.
2: DISAGREE with Claude; adding an import changes mandatory spellings elsewhere.
3: Recommend; stable qualification exposes provenance to auditors. MOD-2/OD139 remain intact; MOD-3 needs revision. OD152 permits declaration-based resolution without a new LL(1) conflict. Philosophy prioritizes transparency over brevity.

ANSWER: PICK=3; DISAGREE=yes; NOTE=Stable qualification makes provenance visible at every use
